* {{sense|dry measure}} {{l|en|cab}}, {{l|en|kab}} {{qualifier|{{frac|180}} homer}}; {{l|en|omer}}, {{l|en|issaron}} {{qualifier|{{frac|100}} homer}}; {{l|en|seah}} {{qualifier|{{frac|30}} homer}}; {{l|en|ephah}} {{qualifier|{{frac|10}} homer}}; {{l|en|lethek}}, {{l|en|lethech}} {{qualifier|{{frac|2}} homer}}
* {{sense|liquid measure}} ''See'' '''{{l|en|cor}}'''

===Etymology 2===
From {{suffix|en|home|er}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|baseball}} A  [[home run]]
#: ''The first baseman hit a '''homer''' to lead off the ninth.''
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[homing pigeon]].
#: ''Each of the pigeon fanciers released a '''homer''' at the same time.''
# {{lb|en|US|sports}} A person who is extremely [[devoted]] to a favorite team.
#: ''Joe is such a '''homer''' that he would never boo the Hometown Hobos, even if they are in last place in the league.''
# A homing beacon.
#* '''1961''', RCA Service Company, ''Digest of Military Electronics'' (page 75)
#*: Two general types of '''homer''' systems are in use, active '''homers''' and passive '''homers'''. Active '''homers''' contain a transmitting device which emits energy that is directed towards the target.

